  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM EST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nVertically stacked low pressure currently over the Northeastern\nU.S. continues to progress downstream from the Great Lakes \nRegion. At the same time. Deep 500mb troughing currently \nbisecting the CONUS will continue to become negatively tilted \nand lift across the eastern half of the country. At the \nsurface\u2013two attached low pressure systems\u2013one over northern \nManitoba and another over the Central Rockies will continue to \ndeepen this weekend before merging into a moderate cyclone again\nover the Northeastern U.S.\n\nLow level troughing attached to the stalled low to our north will \nsupport one or two isolated showers across the CWA this Saturday. \nMore widespread precipitation chances return to the area as the \naforementioned low pressure to our southwest becomes closed and \nspreads light rainfall mainly to the southernmost parts of \nMichigan's Lower Peninsula this Sunday. Conditions then turn cold \nthis Monday as backside cold air advection begins to draw cP air \ninto the region. Precipitation turns back to scattered coverage with \nsnow attempting to mix in as H8 temperatures remain near freezing. \nHigh pressure then builds over the Eastern U.S. the remainder of the \nweek, supporting quiet and benign weather across the Great Lakes \nRegion with temperatures averaging around 10 degrees above normal \nfor early December.\n\nForecast Details: \n\nToday and Sunday: Current showers over Lake Huron will continue to \nmove westward as mostly dry air moves into the region today. The \nvast Majority of the CWA can expect quiet conditions with mostly \ncloudy skies and highs in the 40s. However, enough lingering \nmoisture attached to the aforementioned disheveled trough will sweep \nacross the area and support one or two isolated rain showers. Better \nchances of precipitation build Sunday from deepening low pressure \ntracking across Indiana. Northern lower can expect light stratiform \nrainfall as the deepening cyclone extends its shield into Michigan \nwhile cold air advection commences during the day across Eastern \nUpper. WNW banding should be organized by Sunday evening to produce \nscattered snow showers even with shallow moisture profiles. Places \nthat observe small accumulations can only expect a general tenth or \nso on the ground by Monday morning.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Surface winds veer overnight Sunday into Monday \nas cold air advection from the north begins, continuing lake effect \nshowers across typical snow belts of Northern Michigan. The biggest \nstruggle for snow production will be subsidence quickly replacing \nlow pressure heading downstream and subsequently quickly drying out \nprofiles. Nevertheless, latest guidance does hint at light amounts \nof additional accumulations of another tenth or so for a few areas \nof northern lower and eastern upper before high pressure occupies \nthe central U.S.\n\nWednesday through Friday Outlook: Quiet and benign weather is \nexpected through the remainder of the forecast period as zonal \nmidlevel flow with surface high pressure keeps mostly sunny skies \nover the Central U.S. The 500mb setup remains a split flow pattern \nkeeping cooler air to our north, supporting daytime highs in the 40s \nfor most areas (10 degrees above normal for early December). Long \nrange ensembles depict another low developing over the southwest and \nlifting through the Ohio Valley next weekend which could bring \nactive weather to the Great Lakes next weekend. It is still too \nearly to advertise potential precip types and impacts but we will \ncontinue to monitor this system as we head into early December.",
